      <h2>1. Why Is Talking to Strangers Sometimes Difficult?</h2>

      <p>Talking to an unfamiliar person involves uncertainty. You do not yet know what the other person is interested in, how they will respond, or whether the conversation will continue naturally.</p>

      <h3>1.1 Fear of Negative Evaluation</h3>

      <p>One reason conversations can feel intimidating is the possibility of being judged. People may worry that they will say something embarrassing, appear awkward, or fail to keep a conversation going.</p>

      <p>This concern is different from simply being shy. Persistent and intense fear of social situations can be associated with social anxiety disorder, which is a mental health condition that may require professional assessment.</p>

      <h3>1.2 Lack of Practice</h3>

      <p>Conversation is partly a learned skill. Someone who rarely starts conversations may have fewer opportunities to practice opening questions, follow-up questions, active listening, and handling pauses.</p>

      <h3>1.3 Familiarity Can Reduce Uncertainty</h3>

      <p>Repeatedly encountering a situation can make it feel less unfamiliar. This is one reason structured social practice can be useful for people who want to become more comfortable communicating.</p>

      <h3>1.4 Safety and Social Confidence Are Different</h3>

      <p>Being cautious around strangers is not automatically a confidence problem. Online or offline, people should maintain reasonable safety boundaries while practicing communication.</p>

      <h2>Shyness, Introversion, and Social Anxiety Are Not the Same</h2>

      <p>These terms are often used interchangeably, but they describe different things.</p>

