      <h2>5. Simple Techniques to Build Conversation Confidence</h2>

      <p>You do not need to become extremely talkative to become a better communicator. Focus on specific skills that you can practice during individual conversations.</p>

      <h3>5.1 Start With an Open Question</h3>

      <p>Questions that cannot be answered with only “yes” or “no” give the other person more opportunities to respond.</p>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> “What do you usually enjoy doing on weekends?”</p>

      <h3>5.2 Use Follow-Up Questions</h3>

      <p>Instead of immediately changing subjects, use something the other person already mentioned as your next question.</p>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> If someone says they enjoy photography, ask what type of photography they like.</p>

      <h3>5.3 Listen Instead of Planning Your Next Sentence</h3>

      <p>Good conversation is not a performance. Paying attention to the other person's answer gives you material for the next part of the conversation.</p>

      <h3>5.4 Share Something About Yourself</h3>

      <p>A conversation works in both directions. After asking a question, consider sharing a related experience of your own.</p>

      <h3>5.5 Accept Short Pauses</h3>

      <p>A few seconds of silence do not automatically mean that a conversation has failed. Give yourself and the other person time to think.</p>

      <h3>5.6 Practice Ending Conversations</h3>

      <p>Being able to end a conversation politely is also a communication skill.</p>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> “It was nice talking with you. I’m going to head out now. Have a good day.”</p>

